-- PeaklyPrep: PeaklyTest May 2025 — full practice test from the PeaklyTest library (May 2025 form) --------
-- Generated from the PeaklyTest PDF: questions, answer choices, answer keys and explanations.
-- Figures live in public/q/may25/. Ids are fixed, so running this file again updates the test instead of copying it.

-- PeaklyTest May 2025 · Reading & Writing · Module 1
insert into public.tests (id, title, section, description, duration_min, position, published, form_title, module_no)
values ('4636babb-5131-55d6-b888-9238a1200db8', 'PeaklyTest May 2025 · Reading & Writing · Module 1', 'Reading & Writing', 'Reading & Writing, Module 1 of the May 2025 PeaklyTest · 27 questions · 32 minutes', 32, 220, true, 'PeaklyTest May 2025 · Reading & Writing', 1)
on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, section = excluded.section, description = excluded.description,
  duration_min = excluded.duration_min, position = excluded.position, form_title = excluded.form_title, module_no = excluded.module_no;
insert into public.questions (id, test_id, prompt, options, position, passage) values
  ('93a58c5f-9fec-52f5-9bb2-71dbbc041fc4', '4636babb-5131-55d6-b888-9238a1200db8', 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?', '["mock", "exaggerate", "correct", "undermine"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The Gleaners, painted in the realist style by Jean-François Millet, depicts peasants picking stray wheat from a field after the harvest. The realists'' emphasis on accurately portraying the experiences of average working people was largely a rejection of the romantic style evident in many paintings by Paul Delaroche, which instead _____ their subjects'' beauty or heroism while hiding all imperfection.'),
  ('1ba8c271-210a-51db-b849-b62869d70ddf', '4636babb-5131-55d6-b888-9238a1200db8', 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?', '["substantial", "insurmountable", "feasible", "ambiguous"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The minor planet 1061 Paeonia was named after the plant genus that includes peonies. But most minor planets are given only an identification number, largely due to there being over 500,000 such bodies known at present. So while the task of finding a unique name for each minor planet was _____ when few had been discovered, so many minor planets have since been discovered that the task has become nearly impossible.'),
  ('6293f0a3-1b1e-5489-8997-63f30b4b1082', '4636babb-5131-55d6-b888-9238a1200db8', 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?', '["recur", "dissipate", "concentrate", "terminate"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The term "agglomeration economies" refers to the economic benefits enjoyed by firms in the same industry that _____ in a region. For example, in the computer manufacturing industry in the United Kingdom, firms that locate near one another can more readily take advantage of increased potential for information sharing among firms that fosters greater technological innovation.'),
  ('e5aad22f-f450-5813-b047-d6918eb5c5ac', '4636babb-5131-55d6-b888-9238a1200db8', 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?', '["overcame", "realized", "decried", "evaded"]'::jsonb, 4, 'In the decades after Mexico won its independence from Spain, literature became a medium through which the new nation _____ its autonomous identity, with authors like Manuel Payno and Justo Sierra Méndez helping to shape what would become a distinctly Mexican literary tradition.'),
  ('6b9856e9-3cf6-523e-b31e-8b76cff1c7be', '4636babb-5131-55d6-b888-9238a1200db8', 'Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?', '["It introduces previous findings that suggested a research method for Cruikshank and his team.", "It implies that the research team eliminated an alternative explanation for the crater based on available evidence.", "It emphasizes the importance of the New Horizons space probe to researchers.", "It identifies a misconception that the researchers had about the crater when they were first studying the data."]'::jsonb, 5, 'Researchers examining data from the New Horizons space probe, which passed Pluto in 2015, were puzzled by a 27-mile-long crater on Pluto’s icy surface. Although Pluto is mostly covered in frozen methane and nitrogen, the surface near the crater seemed to show the presence of water ice and ammonia—both of which are associated with eruptions from ice volcanoes. <u>Additionally, the shape and</u> <u>appearance of the crater did not suggest that it was formed by impact.</u> Based on this evidence, scientist Dale Cruikshank and his team hypothesized that the crater was likely once a massive ice volcano.'),
  ('ac2c858f-ba9b-50c6-a703-078038be991d', '4636babb-5131-55d6-b888-9238a1200db8', 'Based on the texts, the author of Text 1 and the author of Text 2 would most likely agree with which statement about many new albums?', '["Many new albums include more artwork than albums generally once included.", "Many new albums sell more copies than albums generally once sold.", "Many new albums are longer than albums generally once were.", "Many new albums are quieter than albums generally once were."]'::jsonb, 6, '**Text 1**
In the past, music albums were created to be played back on physical formats like records. A typical record holds only about twenty-five minutes of music on each of its shiny black sides. This forced artists and producers to think carefully about what music to include on an album and what to leave out. Today’s artists don’t have to worry as much about fitting their music on physical formats. For this reason, many of today’s artists are making albums that are much longer than albums from decades past.

**Text 2**
In today’s digital age, most music is created to be streamed from online services. These services remove the limitations of physical formats like records and CDs. This may be why many new albums are longer and have more songs than albums crafted for physical formats. Unfortunately, many of these longer albums suffer from a lack of focus.'),
  ('f2d5aa7e-f52c-511a-9fbd-8dd187a83445', '4636babb-5131-55d6-b888-9238a1200db8', 'Based on the text, what piece of knowledge about the Pledge of Allegiance has recently become uncertain?', '["How many changes were made to the pledge in the 1900s.", "Which part of the pledge students liked best in 1892.", "The name of the newspaper that first published the pledge.", "The identity of the person who first wrote the pledge."]'::jsonb, 7, 'It has long been thought that the original author of the US Pledge of Allegiance was Francis Bellamy, who said that he wrote the pledge one evening in August of 1892. But a historian recently discovered a newspaper article that was published several months before August 1892. The article describes students reciting the same pledge that Bellamy claims he first wrote in August. This means that Bellamy may not have created the pledge after all.'),
  ('59fbc8fd-393a-505e-ad24-a5b92c92ff72', '4636babb-5131-55d6-b888-9238a1200db8', 'Which choice best states the main idea of the text?', '["Cuttlefish eyes are much more complex than those of either sheep or macaque monkeys.", "Before completing their study, Wardill’s team showed that sheep have 3D vision, but macaque monkeys may not.", "Wardill’s team designed 3D glasses for cuttlefish.", "The findings of Wardill’s team’s study suggest that like sheep and macaque monkeys, cuttlefish may also have 3D vision."]'::jsonb, 8, 'Sheep and macaque monkeys can see in three dimensions (3D), which helps them perceive distance and depth. Octopuses and squid are thought to lack 3D vision. Many researchers once thought the same about cuttlefish, but Trevor Wardill and his team wanted to test whether this assumption was true. The team studied how cuttlefish wearing 3D glasses reacted to 3D images of shrimp (a favorite prey) projected on a tank wall. Cuttlefish changed their striking position to match the 3D images, suggesting that their vision is more like that of sheep and macaque monkeys than that of octopuses or squid.'),
  ('33021689-cede-546a-9451-cebbf35d91e4', '4636babb-5131-55d6-b888-9238a1200db8', 'What does the text most strongly suggest about Tom Towers?', '["He has received much praise but tends to place too much value on his own importance.", "He takes private satisfaction in his capacity to influence public opinion.", "He aims mainly to win the regard of readers who reside in other provinces.", "He flatters himself that his readers take a greater interest in him than in politicians."]'::jsonb, 9, 'The following text is from Anthony Trollope’s 1855 novel The Warden. Tom Towers is a writer for the newspaper The Jupiter. It is true that in far-off provinces men did not talk daily of Tom Towers but they read The Jupiter, and acknowledged that without The Jupiter life was not worth having. This kind of hidden but still conscious glory suited the nature of the man. He loved to sit silent in a corner of his club and listen to the loud chattering of politicians, and to think how they all were in his power;--how he could smite the loudest of them, were it worth his while to raise his pen for such a purpose.'),
  ('12b6d877-f1c8-5d41-bbae-079a964edef2', '4636babb-5131-55d6-b888-9238a1200db8', 'Which quotation from Life Among the Paiutes most effectively illustrates the claim?', '["\"During the time my grandfather was away in California, where he [stayed] till after the Mexican war, there was a girl-baby born in our family.\"", "\"Now, my dear reader, there is no word so endearing as the word father, and that is why [my people] call all good people father or mother.\"", "\"But how can I describe the scene that followed? Some of you, dear readers, can imagine.\"", "\"We would all go in company to see if the flowers we were named for were yet in bloom, for almost all the girls are named for flowers.\""]'::jsonb, 10, 'Life Among the Paiutes is an 1882 autobiographical narrative by Sarah Winnemucca Hopkins, a Northern Paiute author, educator, and activist. In the work, Winnemucca directly addresses the reader to explain certain customs, writing _____'),
  ('238f53ba-7ff3-5987-978c-4ad7391eb7f0', '4636babb-5131-55d6-b888-9238a1200db8', 'Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the example?', '["0.7905.", "-0.3238.", "-3.2570.", "0.4729."]'::jsonb, 11, 'Effect of Neighboring Species on Pollinator Visits to Target Species

Effect of Neighboring Species on Pollinator Visits to Target Species

| Neighboring species | Target species | Effect value |
| leafy spurge | Lewis flax | -0.3238 |
| Himalayan balsam | marsh woundwort | 0.7905 |
| Canadian wood betony | mayapple | 0.4729 |

Researchers Carolina Laura Morales and Anna Traveset gathered data about flowering plants growing alongside each other in various locations. In each case, the researchers identified one plant as a "target species" and a nearby plant as a "neighboring species." The researchers then calculated a value to show how the neighboring species affected pollinator visits to the target species. The table shows that the Himalayan balsam had an effect value of _____'),
  ('759c078f-3eac-54c6-b0f6-309dc24c95a4', '4636babb-5131-55d6-b888-9238a1200db8', 'Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the comparison?', '["both accommodation/food services and retail but below health care.", "accommodation/food services but below both health care and retail.", "health care, retail, and accommodation/food services.", "accommodation/food services and nearly equal to health care and retail."]'::jsonb, 12, '| Industry | Approximate total contribution by industry | Number of people employed by industry | Average contribution per employee by industry |
| Accommodation/food services | \$5,242,100,000 | 150,373 | \$34,861 |
| Tribal economic activity | \$7,312,400,000 | 51,674 | \$141,510 |
| Health care | \$13,727,300,000 | 193,514 | \$70,937 |
| Retail | \$10,738,800,000 | 179,208 | \$59,924 |

The nearly forty tribes located in Oklahoma, including the Chickasaw Nation and the Seminole Nation, operate numerous businesses and generate billions of dollars in revenue. A student in an economics class is researching the tribes'' collective activity as a single industry. The student wants to compare that industry’s contribution to Oklahoma’s overall economy in 2017 with the contributions made by three other industries in the state. Looking at the table, the student finds that tribal economic activity totaled over 7.3 billion USD, ranking it above ______.'),
  ('bf31fe2f-f912-56d6-b811-9ec728492818', '4636babb-5131-55d6-b888-9238a1200db8', 'Which detail, if true, would most directly support the team’s conclusion?', '["Most studies of microplastic accumulation on leaves have utilized extraction methods that do not have a substantial effect on epicuticular wax.", "Most studies of microplastic accumulation on leaves have focused on forests with diverse tree species that exhibit varying levels of epicuticular wax.", "Most studies of microplastic accumulation on leaves have found particulate concentrations that greatly exceeded 53.8 percent.", "Most studies of microplastic accumulation on leaves have involved the application of alkaline treatments before water and ultrasonic treatments rather than after them."]'::jsonb, 13, 'Assessing the role of forests as sinks for airborne microplastics that would otherwise continue to disperse in the atmosphere, Akane Miyazaki and her team measured concentrations of plastic particulates on leaves from konara oak trees in Japan. The team used three separate extraction methods: an ultrapure water rinse, a rinse combined with ultrasonic waves, and an alkaline solution treatment that removes epicuticular wax (a surface coating on most land plants to which particles can adhere). The concentrations of particulates recovered were 7.6 percent for water extraction, 38.4 percent for ultrasonic extraction, and 53.8 percent for alkaline extraction, leading the team to conclude that previous assessments have likely underestimated forest canopies'' capacity as sinks.'),
  ('976306f6-d997-5e98-86f2-89572b54f1a6', '4636babb-5131-55d6-b888-9238a1200db8', 'Which choice most logically completes the text?', '["it may be advantageous for music streaming companies to try to influence the extent to which potential users perceive the premium version as pleasant to use.", "participants who strongly agreed with the statement \"Using paid music streaming services is pleasant\" were more likely to express loyalty to Apple Music than to other streaming services.", "the statement \"Using paid music streaming services is pleasant\" is not a reliable measure of hedonic motivation.", "users'' intentions to pay for premium streaming services are probably unaffected by factors other than hedonic motivation."]'::jsonb, 14, 'Mariana Lopes Barata and Pedro Simões Coelho collected data from 324 music-streaming service users to identify factors that influence users to opt for paid (premium) versions of music streaming services, like Apple Music. They hypothesized that hedonic motivation (the enjoyment that a consumer perceives to be associated with using a service) would be positively correlated with users'' intentions to adopt premium versions. The researchers asked participants to rate statements such as "Using paid music streaming services is pleasant." They found that, indeed, hedonic motivation positively influences the intention to adopt premium streaming service versions, which suggests that _____'),
  ('336343ed-b63d-5cc4-9dca-b77f3981acfe', '4636babb-5131-55d6-b888-9238a1200db8', 'Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?', '["will publish", "published", "publishes", "is publishing"]'::jsonb, 15, 'On August 15, 2013, the Navajo Times _____ a profile of Manuelito Chapter (Kín Hózhóní), which is one of the 110 chapters of the Navajo Nation (Naałeehó Bínáhásdzó). The article was later included in a book series called Exploring the Navajo Nation Chapter by Chapter.'),
  ('66ee99bd-a9ec-5ed0-ba2f-0b19cf095336', '4636babb-5131-55d6-b888-9238a1200db8', 'Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?', '["1804", "1804—", "1804;", "1804:"]'::jsonb, 16, 'According to the original text of the US Constitution, written in 1787, the presidential candidate receiving the second-most Electoral College votes becomes vice president. The 12th amendment, ratified in _____ separated the elections for the two offices.'),
  ('d453875e-312e-51bf-8407-361d907eb1a5', '4636babb-5131-55d6-b888-9238a1200db8', 'Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?', '["artist’s over 160 year''s", "artists over 160 years''", "artists'' over 160 years", "artists over 160 years"]'::jsonb, 17, 'The colors of Navajo (Diné) weaver Melissa Cody’s work World Traveler (2014) recall both the Germantown revival, a weaving style developed by Navajo Nation _____ ago, and the color palette of the video games that Cody played as a child.'),
  ('0c5a4f82-c115-5689-8273-0cd0fbdc169d', '4636babb-5131-55d6-b888-9238a1200db8', 'Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?', '["essayist Carolyn Servid", "essayist, Carolyn Servid", "essayist Carolyn Servid,", "essayist, Carolyn Servid,"]'::jsonb, 18, 'The editors of Home Ground: A Guide to the American Landscape turned to Luis Alberto Urrea, a poet, to craft the entry for "atarque," a term referring to a watering hole formed by a natural or artificial dam. For "muskeg," however, the editors chose the _____ author of Of Landscape and Longing.'),
  ('4b9d4e2f-ed2f-5db1-bf80-70bb62e0265b', '4636babb-5131-55d6-b888-9238a1200db8', 'Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?', '["Hilma af Klint, well before many of her contemporaries, explored abstract modes of painting,", "Hilma af Klint explored abstract modes of painting well before many of her contemporaries,", "well before many of her contemporaries, Hilma af Klint explored abstract modes of painting,", "the explorations of Hilma af Klint into abstract modes of painting occured well before those of many of her contemporaries,"]'::jsonb, 19, 'A groundbreaking visual artist of her day, ______ including artists Vasily Kandinsky and Piet Mondrian.'),
  ('9007b7e8-25d5-5e24-85e7-e7dee5543380', '4636babb-5131-55d6-b888-9238a1200db8', 'Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?', '["astronomers modeling of the objects", "astronomer’s modeling of the object''s", "astronomers'' modeling of the object''s", "astronomers modeling of the object''s"]'::jsonb, 20, 'The small celestial body known as 2010 BL4 is expected to have a relatively short lifetime in the outer solar system. Within just a few million years, 2010 BL4 is likely to migrate into the inner solar system or be destroyed, according to _____ trajectory.'),
  ('2e3a3ef3-fc2b-5700-b419-7ea850cfcc9d', '4636babb-5131-55d6-b888-9238a1200db8', 'Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?', '["despair, though", "despair, though,", "despair, though;", "despair—though"]'::jsonb, 21, 'Many works of the Roman scholar Varro (1st century BCE), including his history of Rome, De gente populi Romani, have been lost to time. Scholars wishing to read a work written by the Roman scholar needn’t _____ they can still procure a copy of Varro’s Rerum rusticarum libra tres, a treatise on agriculture.'),
  ('cc9c85a7-78b0-5d08-9ece-3038f61af2a1', '4636babb-5131-55d6-b888-9238a1200db8', 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?', '["Similarly,", "Afterward,", "Hence,", "Specifically,"]'::jsonb, 22, 'Using LiDAR (Light Detection and Ranging) technology, Tulane University archaeologists scanned and mapped the ancient Mayan city of Tikal, which lies deep in the Guatemalan rainforest. Some of Tikal’s old stones are intertwined with the roots of surrounding trees, rendering parts of the complex physically inaccessible to researchers. _____ the LiDAR’s penetrating light waves were the only way to reveal some previously unknown aspects of the Mayan city.'),
  ('cde0df2d-d87c-527f-a1b8-fd7bc94c22d6', '4636babb-5131-55d6-b888-9238a1200db8', 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?', '["For example,", "Specifically,", "By contrast,", "As a result,"]'::jsonb, 23, 'Imagine a magazine that a reader has thrown away. This magazine is post-consumer waste, as it became waste after reaching the consumer. _____ the paper scraps left over from printing the magazine are pre-consumer waste, as they became waste before reaching the consumer.'),
  ('abd5300c-e2a9-50c7-962d-04a07da64274', '4636babb-5131-55d6-b888-9238a1200db8', 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?', '["Regardless,", "For instance,", "Granted,", "Instead,"]'::jsonb, 24, 'One proposed boundary between Earth’s atmosphere and outer space is the Kármán line, 100 km above sea level. Based on the work of physicist Theodore von Kármán, this line marks the theoretical height at which an aircraft no longer remains aloft using the force of lift. _____ an aircraft sustains flight past this altitude primarily by its velocity, reaching a speed sufficient to maintain an orbit but not to generate enough lift from the thin air.'),
  ('caef1381-9ad0-50e8-ad3d-a12f7a730ae8', '4636babb-5131-55d6-b888-9238a1200db8', 'The student wants to indicate the year the Schomburg Center was founded. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?', '["The Schomburg Center was named for Arturo Alfonso Schomburg.", "The Schomburg Center is a research library.", "The Schomburg Center was founded in 1925.", "The Schomburg Center is located in New York City."]'::jsonb, 25, 'While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:

The Schomburg Center for Research in Black Culture is a research library in New York City. It was named for Arturo Alfonso Schomburg.
It was founded in 1925.'),
  ('49e3f748-ce15-5be7-9ba9-44abbc245d27', '4636babb-5131-55d6-b888-9238a1200db8', 'The student wants to compare the vitamin C content of brussels sprouts and apples. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?', '["Brussels sprouts contain vitamin C (also known as ascorbic acid)—80 mg in every 100 g, to be exact.", "Brussels sprouts and apples both contain vitamin C, but brussels sprouts contain more.", "Brussels sprouts are vegetables, and apples are fruits.", "Humans cannot make ascorbic acid in their bodies, but they can get it from brussels sprouts."]'::jsonb, 26, 'While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:

Brussels sprouts are vegetables that contain ascorbic acid, an essential nutrient for humans. Apples are fruits that contain ascorbic acid.
There is 80 milligrams (mg) of ascorbic acid in every 100 grams (g) of brussels sprouts. There is 6 mg of ascorbic acid in every 100 g of apple.
Humans cannot make ascorbic acid in their bodies, so they must get it from foods, including fruits and vegetables.
Ascorbic acid is also known as vitamin C.'),
  ('02da3112-2b5d-5f8c-867d-5dd9693000cf', '4636babb-5131-55d6-b888-9238a1200db8', 'The student wants to emphasize a similarity between the two films. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?', '["The Three Passions and Désirée are both films that include Merle Oberon, the first Indian-born actress to be nominated for an Academy Award.", "The Three Passions (1928) was released early in actress Merle Oberon’s career, whereas Désirée (1954) came out later.", "In The Three Passions (1928), actress Merle Oberon played a nameless, uncredited role; however, in Désirée (1954), she played a credited role—that of Empress Josephine.", "Early in her career, Merle Oberon wasn’t listed in some film credits, such as the credits for the film The Three Passions, where she played a nameless, uncredited role."]'::jsonb, 27, 'While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:

Merle Oberon (1911–1979) was an actress born in Mumbai (then known as Bombay), India. She was of Indian, Maori, and Irish heritage.
She was the first Indian-born actress to be nominated for an Academy Award.
Early in her career, she played many nameless, uncredited roles, such as her role in The Three Passions (1928).
Later, she played many named, credited roles, such as Empress Josephine in Désirée (1954).')
on conflict (id) do update set test_id = excluded.test_id, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options,
  position = excluded.position, passage = excluded.passage;
insert into public.question_keys (question_id, correct_index, correct_answers, explanation) values
  ('93a58c5f-9fec-52f5-9bb2-71dbbc041fc4', 1, null, '**Choice B is correct.** The sentence contrasts the realists’ accurate portrayals with the romantic style; the phrase “while hiding all imperfection” signals that the romantic painters presented subjects as more perfect or grand than they really were. To “exaggerate their subjects’ beauty or heroism” matches that meaning precisely.
**Choice A is incorrect.** To “mock” means to make fun of or ridicule, which contradicts the idea of hiding imperfections and elevating subjects.
**Choice C is incorrect.** To “correct” would imply fixing flaws, which doesn’t fit the context of making subjects appear more ideal than they are.
**Choice D is incorrect.** To “undermine” means to weaken or sabotage, which conflicts with the notion of enhancing beauty or heroism.
**Faster approach:** Use the contrast signal “instead” and the clause “hiding all imperfection” to eliminate words with opposite meanings (mock, undermine) and select the option that conveys enhancement—here, “exaggerate.”'),
  ('1ba8c271-210a-51db-b849-b62869d70ddf', 2, null, '**Choice C is correct.** The sentence contrasts the earlier situation (few minor planets discovered) with the present situation (so many discovered that naming them is nearly impossible). The blank therefore needs a word meaning “possible” or “doable” in the earlier situation. "Feasible" precisely conveys that the task was manageable when there were only a few discoveries, so it fits the contrast and logical progression.
**Choice A is incorrect.** "Substantial" means large or significant; it does not convey whether the task was possible or not, so it fails to produce the intended contrast with "nearly impossible."
**Choice B is incorrect.** "Insurmountable" means impossible to overcome. That would contradict the idea that the task was manageable when there were few discoveries, so it is the wrong sense.
**Choice D is incorrect.** "Ambiguous" means unclear or open to multiple interpretations; it does not convey that the task could be done when few planets had been discovered, so it does not create the necessary contrast.
**Faster approach:** Identify the contrast markers ("while...when...so many...that...nearly impossible") and pick the antonym of "nearly impossible" — here, "feasible."'),
  ('6293f0a3-1b1e-5489-8997-63f30b4b1082', 2, null, '**Choice C is correct.** The term "agglomeration economies" describes benefits when firms in the same industry are gathered in one place, so the sentence requires a verb meaning "gather together" or "be located in close proximity." "Concentrate" precisely conveys that idea: firms that concentrate in a region can share information and spur innovation.
**Choice A is incorrect.** "Recur" means to happen again or repeatedly; it does not describe firms being located together and thus does not fit the context of geographic clustering.
**Choice B is incorrect.** "Dissipate" means to scatter or disappear, the opposite of what agglomeration economies describe, so it contradicts the passage.
**Choice D is incorrect.** "Terminate" means to end; it does not convey firms gathering in a region and so is not appropriate here.
**Faster approach:** Look for a word that means "gather together" or "cluster" to match the idea of firms located near one another; eliminate words that mean "scatter," "end," or "repeat."'),
  ('e5aad22f-f450-5813-b047-d6918eb5c5ac', 1, null, '**Choice B is correct.** The sentence describes literature as a medium through which a newly independent nation developed or brought into being an autonomous identity; "realized" precisely conveys the idea of coming to possess or recognize that identity. The context indicates a positive, constructive process (authors "helping to shape" a tradition), so a verb meaning "achieved" or "brought about" fits best.
**Choice A is incorrect.** "Overcame" requires an obstacle as its object (e.g., "overcame challenges") and does not logically take "its autonomous identity" as something to be overcome.
**Choice C is incorrect.** "Decried" means "publicly denounced," which contradicts the context that authors were shaping and promoting a Mexican literary tradition.
**Choice D is incorrect.** "Evaded" means "avoided" or "escaped," which conflicts with the idea of literature serving to form or express an identity.
**Faster approach:** Substitute each choice into the sentence and ask whether the verb means "to develop or bring into being" (fits the positive, formative context). That quickly eliminates verbs like "overcame," "decried," and "evaded," leaving "realized."'),
  ('6b9856e9-3cf6-523e-b31e-8b76cff1c7be', 1, null, '**Choice B is correct.** The underlined sentence states that the crater’s "shape and appearance... did not suggest that it was formed by impact," which directly indicates that the researchers rejected an impact-origin explanation. This supports the passage’s presentation of evidence leading Cruikshank and his team to hypothesize a volcanic origin, so the sentence functions to show elimination of an alternative explanation.
**Choice A is incorrect.** The sentence does not introduce prior findings or a research method; it reports an observation that rules out a specific cause.
**Choice C is incorrect.** The sentence makes no claim about the importance of the New Horizons probe; it refers only to the crater’s appearance.
**Choice D is incorrect.** The sentence does not describe a misconception the researchers had; rather, it describes an observation that led them to dismiss an impact hypothesis.
**Faster approach:** Look for language that negates or rules out an explanation (e.g., "did not suggest"); such phrases typically indicate elimination of alternatives and point to choice B.'),
  ('ac2c858f-ba9b-50c6-a703-078038be991d', 2, null, '**Choice C is correct.** Both texts state that contemporary albums tend to be longer than older albums because digital distribution removes the time limits of physical formats. Text 1 says artists “are making albums that are much longer than albums from decades past,” and Text 2 says “many new albums are longer and have more songs than albums crafted for physical formats,” directly supporting choice C.
**Choice A is incorrect.** Neither passage mentions album artwork or claims that new albums include more artwork than older albums.
**Choice B is incorrect.** Neither passage makes any claim about sales or the number of copies sold of new albums versus older albums.
**Choice D is incorrect.** The passages do not discuss volume or quietness of music; they address length and the number of songs, not loudness.
**Faster approach:** Scan both passages for repeated specific claims; the shared statement about length (longer albums) appears in both, so choose the option that directly restates that claim.'),
  ('f2d5aa7e-f52c-511a-9fbd-8dd187a83445', 3, null, '**Choice D is correct.** The passage explains that Francis Bellamy had long been thought to have written the Pledge of Allegiance, but the discovery of a newspaper article published before Bellamy’s claimed date suggests he may not have written it. That directly casts doubt on the identity of the person who first wrote the pledge.
**Choice A is incorrect.** The passage says nothing about how many changes were made to the pledge in the 1900s; it only discusses who may have written the pledge originally.
**Choice B is incorrect.** The passage mentions students reciting the pledge but provides no information about students’ preferences or which part they liked best.
**Choice C is incorrect.** The passage refers to a newspaper article but does not say that the newspaper’s name is in question; the key uncertainty is about the pledge’s authorship.
**Faster approach:** For central-idea questions, find the sentence that states a change or contradiction (here, the discovery that predates Bellamy’s claim) and match that change to the answer choice that refers to who or what is now uncertain.'),
  ('59fbc8fd-393a-505e-ad24-a5b92c92ff72', 3, null, '**Choice D is correct.** The passage explains that researchers tested whether cuttlefish have 3D vision by using 3D images of shrimp and observing that cuttlefish adjusted their striking position to match those images, which supports the conclusion that cuttlefish may have 3D vision like sheep and macaque monkeys.
**Choice A is incorrect.** The passage compares 3D vision capabilities, not the overall complexity of cuttlefish eyes relative to sheep or macaques.
**Choice B is incorrect.** The passage states sheep and macaque monkeys can see in 3D; it does not say Wardill’s team showed this or suggest macaques may lack 3D vision.
**Choice C is incorrect.** While the study used 3D images and glasses, the main idea is the study’s finding about cuttlefish vision, not the fact that the team designed glasses.
**Faster approach:** For main-idea questions, restate the passage in one sentence and choose the answer that matches that central conclusion; eliminate choices focused on minor details.'),
  ('33021689-cede-546a-9451-cebbf35d91e4', 1, null, '**Choice B is correct.** The passage describes Tom Towers’ “hidden but still conscious glory” and says he “loved to sit silent” and imagine how politicians “were in his power” and how he could “smite” them with his pen. Those details show that he privately takes satisfaction in his ability to influence public opinion rather than seeking overt praise.
**Choice A is incorrect.** The passage does not say he has received much praise; it emphasizes a quiet, hidden pride, not widespread acclaim, so the first part is unsupported.
**Choice C is incorrect.** Although the passage mentions readers in “far-off provinces,” it does not suggest his main aim is to win their regard.
**Choice D is incorrect.** The passage indicates he feels power over politicians, not that he believes readers are more interested in him than in politicians.
**Faster approach:** Look for language that describes internal feelings or imagined control (words like “hidden,” “conscious,” “in his power”) to identify private satisfaction in influence—this reliably points to answer B.'),
  ('12b6d877-f1c8-5d41-bbae-079a964edef2', 1, null, '**Choice B is correct.** The quotation explicitly addresses the audience with "Now, my dear reader," and immediately explains a cultural practice: that the Paiutes call all good people "father or mother." This both directly addresses the reader and clarifies a specific custom, matching the prompt.
**Choice A is incorrect.** Although factual, it is a family narrative about a birth and does not directly address the reader or explain a custom.
**Choice C is incorrect.** It does directly address the reader, but it asks readers to imagine a scene rather than explaining a cultural practice.
**Choice D is incorrect.** This sentence explains a custom (naming girls for flowers) but does not directly address the reader in the way the prompt requires.
**Faster approach:** Scan each quotation for explicit second-person phrases (e.g., "my dear reader," "you") and then check whether that sentence also gives a reason or explanation of a cultural practice; the one that contains both is the correct choice.'),
  ('238f53ba-7ff3-5987-978c-4ad7391eb7f0', 0, null, '**Choice A is correct.** The table lists the neighboring species "Himalayan balsam" with the target species "marsh woundwort" and an effect value of $0.7905$, so that is the value that completes the sentence.
**Choice B is incorrect.** The value $-0.3238$ in the table corresponds to leafy spurge affecting Lewis flax, not Himalayan balsam.
**Choice C is incorrect.** The value $-3.2570$ does not appear anywhere in the table and therefore is not supported by the provided data.
**Choice D is incorrect.** The value $0.4729$ corresponds to Canadian wood betony affecting mayapple, not to Himalayan balsam.
**Faster approach:** Scan the table for the row labeled "Himalayan balsam" and read the effect value in that row —this directly yields $0.7905$.'),
  ('759c078f-3eac-54c6-b0f6-309dc24c95a4', 1, null, '**Choice B is correct.** The table shows tribal economic activity at $7,312,400,000$, which is greater than accommodation/food services at $5,242,100,000$ but less than both retail ($10,738,800,000$) and health care ( $13,727,300,000$). Thus tribal activity ranks above accommodation/food services but below the other two.
**Choice A is incorrect.** That choice says tribal activity is above both accommodation/food services and retail but below health care, but tribal ($7.31B$) is below retail ($10.74B$), so it cannot be above retail.
**Choice C is incorrect.** That choice claims tribal activity is above all three industries, which is false because tribal is smaller than retail and health care.
**Choice D is incorrect.** Tribal activity is not nearly equal to health care or retail; those industries are substantially larger (about $13.7B$ and $10.7B$ respectively).
**Faster approach:** Directly compare the dollar amounts in the table—identify which numbers are larger or smaller than $7,312,400,000$ to place tribal activity relative to each industry.'),
  ('bf31fe2f-f912-56d6-b811-9ec728492818', 0, null, '**Choice A is correct.** The researchers recovered much more particulate matter when they used an alkaline treatment that removed epicuticular wax (53.8%) than when they used only water (7.6%) or ultrasonic extraction (38.4%), so if most prior studies used methods that did not substantially affect epicuticular wax, those studies would likely have missed particles adhering to the wax and therefore underestimated canopy sink capacity.
**Choice B is incorrect.** Variation among species in wax levels might introduce variability, but it does not most directly explain systematic underestimation; the key is the extraction method’s inability to remove wax-bound particles.
**Choice C is incorrect.** If prior studies found concentrations exceeding 53.8%, that would contradict the team’s claim of underestimation, not support it.
**Choice D is incorrect.** If prior studies applied alkaline treatment first, they would have recovered more particles (not fewer), so that practice would not explain underestimation by earlier work.
**Faster approach:** Focus on which answer links the new method’s specific advantage (removing wax and recovering more particles) to a common feature of prior studies—choose the option describing prior methods that would have missed wax-bound particles.'),
  ('976306f6-d997-5e98-86f2-89572b54f1a6', 0, null, '**Choice A is correct.** The study found that hedonic motivation (perceiving the service as pleasant) positively influences intentions to adopt premium versions, so it is logical to conclude that companies could benefit from increasing users'' perceptions of pleasantness to encourage upgrades.
**Choice B is incorrect.** The passage reports a general relationship between hedonic motivation and adoption intentions; it does not provide any data comparing loyalty to Apple Music versus other services.
**Choice C is incorrect.** The researchers used the statement as a measure and found a positive relationship; nothing in the passage suggests the item is unreliable.
**Choice D is incorrect.** The finding that hedonic motivation influences intentions does not imply that other factors have no effect; the passage does not support such an absolute claim.
**Faster approach:** Restate the finding in plain terms and choose the option that logically follows (practical implication) rather than one that introduces unsupported specifics or absolutes.'),
  ('336343ed-b63d-5cc4-9dca-b77f3981acfe', 1, null, '**Choice B is correct.** The sentence begins with a specific past date, "On August 15, 2013," which requires a past-tense verb to show that the action occurred at that time. The simple past verb "published" correctly indicates that the Navajo Times ran the profile on that date.
**Choice A is incorrect.** "Will publish" is future tense and conflicts with the past date given in the sentence.
**Choice C is incorrect.** "Publishes" (simple present) is used for habitual actions or general truths, not for a single event tied to a specific past date.
**Choice D is incorrect.** "Is publishing" (present progressive) describes an action happening now or around the present, which contradicts the 2013 date.
**Faster approach:** For any sentence that opens with a specific past date (e.g., "On August 15, 2013,"), immediately choose a simple past verb form to match that time marker.'),
  ('66ee99bd-a9ec-5ed0-ba2f-0b19cf095336', 0, null, '**Choice A is correct.** The phrase "ratified in 1804" is a parenthetical (nonrestrictive) modifier that should be set off from the rest of the sentence by neutral punctuation; the only acceptable insertion from the choices is the plain year, "1804," because the other choices add incompatible punctuation marks.
**Choice B is incorrect.** An em dash is too strong and would create an abrupt break that is not warranted for a simple parenthetical date.
**Choice C is incorrect.** A semicolon cannot follow a year here because semicolons link independent clauses, and the clause after the date is not independent.
**Choice D is incorrect.** A colon introduces a list or explanation; it is not appropriate immediately after a parenthetical date within this sentence.
**Faster approach:** Recognize that a year in a parenthetical modifier should appear as the bare year (no colon, semicolon, or dash). Always choose the plain year when the only alternatives add inappropriate punctuation.'),
  ('d453875e-312e-51bf-8407-361d907eb1a5', 3, null, '**Choice D is correct.** The sentence requires the phrase “artists over 160 years ago” to indicate when Navajo Nation artists developed the Germantown revival. Placing “ago” after the numeral phrase is the standard, and no possession is needed: “developed by Navajo Nation artists over 160 years ago” is grammatical and clear.
**Choice A is incorrect.** “artist’s over 160 year''s” uses two incorrect possessive forms; neither the artists nor the years should be possessive in this construction.
**Choice B is incorrect.** “artists over 160 years''” places an unnecessary possessive apostrophe after “years,” which is ungrammatical here.
**Choice C is incorrect.** “artists'' over 160 years” makes “artists” possessive, which is unnecessary and incorrect for this passive development phrase.
**Faster approach:** Read the sentence aloud with each option; the correct choice will sound like a straightforward time phrase ending with “ago” (no apostrophes or possessives).'),
  ('0c5a4f82-c115-5689-8273-0cd0fbdc169d', 2, null, '**Choice C is correct.** The sentence reads “...the editors chose the _____ author of Of Landscape and Longing.” The word group that fits best is a title plus a following appositive: “essayist Carolyn Servid, author of Of Landscape and Longing.” When a title immediately precedes a name, no comma separates them, and a comma is required after the name to set off the appositive phrase that follows.
**Choice A is incorrect.** It omits the comma after the name, yielding “essayist Carolyn Servid author of...,” which fails to mark the appositive and is ungrammatical.
**Choice B is incorrect.** The comma before the name (“essayist, Carolyn Servid”) incorrectly separates the title from the name; titles that precede names are not set off by a comma.
**Choice D is incorrect.** Commas both before and after the name would set the name off parenthetically, which is not the convention when a title directly precedes a name; the preferred form places no comma before the name.
**Faster approach:** Identify whether the phrase after the blank is an appositive; if so, leave no comma between a title and the name, and place a comma after the name to set off the appositive—eliminate choices with incorrect comma placement.'),
  ('4b9d4e2f-ed2f-5db1-bf80-70bb62e0265b', 1, null, '**Choice B is correct.** The sentence reads smoothly as: “A groundbreaking visual artist of her day, Hilma af Klint explored abstract modes of painting well before many of her contemporaries, including artists Vasily Kandinsky and Piet Mondrian.” The introductory appositive correctly modifies Hilma af Klint, the main clause is grammatical, and the phrase “including artists Vasily Kandinsky and Piet Mondrian” immediately follows and clearly modifies “many of her contemporaries.”
**Choice A is incorrect.** Placing commas around “well before many of her contemporaries” makes that phrase parenthetical and leaves “including artists Vasily Kandinsky and Piet Mondrian” awkwardly attached to “painting,” which would illogically modify a verb with people.
**Choice C is incorrect.** Beginning with “well before many of her contemporaries” separates that noun phrase from the modifying “including” phrase, creating ambiguity and less clear modification.
**Choice D is incorrect.** The phrasing is awkward and unidiomatic, and “occured” is misspelled; it also creates a heavy, wordy construction where a simpler active verb is preferable.
**Faster approach:** Place the “including” phrase next to the noun it modifies (here, “many of her contemporaries”); eliminate choices that separate them or create illogical attachments.'),
  ('9007b7e8-25d5-5e24-85e7-e7dee5543380', 2, null, '**Choice C is correct.** The sentence needs a plural possessive to show that the modeling belongs to the astronomers and then must link that modeling to the object’s trajectory, which appears immediately after the blank: "according to astronomers'' modeling of the object’s trajectory." The plural possessive "astronomers''" correctly modifies "modeling," and "of the object''s" combines with the following word "trajectory" to form the correct possessive phrase.
**Choice A is incorrect.** It lacks the possessive apostrophe after "astronomers," and it uses the plural noun "objects," which does not fit the singular "2010 BL4."
**Choice B is incorrect.** It uses the singular possessive "astronomer''s," which would imply a single astronomer; the context implies multiple researchers, and the intended meaning is better conveyed by the plural possessive.
**Choice D is incorrect.** It omits the possessive apostrophe after "astronomers," so it does not show that the modeling belongs to the astronomers.
**Faster approach:** Look for possessive needs: place the apostrophe on the person(s) doing the action (astronomers'') and ensure the noun after "of" will link to the following word ("object''s" + "trajectory").'),
  ('2e3a3ef3-fc2b-5700-b419-7ea850cfcc9d', 2, null, '**Choice C is correct.** The sentence uses a main clause followed by a parenthetical adverbial phrase "though" that contrasts with it; that parenthetical should be set off from the main clause with commas. Here the phrase "Scholars wishing to read a work written by the Roman scholar needn’t despair" is the main clause, and the contrasting adverbial "though" follows it; the comma before and after "though" is required to indicate the parenthetical. Choice C supplies the comma after "though," correctly punctuating the parenthetical at the end of the sentence.
**Choice A is incorrect.** It places a comma only before "though" but omits the necessary closing comma after "though," leaving the parenthetical unclosed and producing a punctuation imbalance.
**Choice B is incorrect.** It places a comma after "despair" and another comma after "though," yielding two commas in a row ("despair, though,") which is incorrect punctuation: the parenthetical should be "though" set off by commas, but placing an extra comma before "though" creates an unnecessary and incorrect sequence.
**Choice D is incorrect.** A dash before "though" would create a stronger break than the sentence requires and would not be paired with a closing dash; also, using an em dash here would interrupt the flow improperly—standard punctuation for a short parenthetical adverb at the end of a sentence is commas, not a dash.
**Faster approach:** For deciding punctuation around short parenthetical words like "however," "though," or "indeed," scan whether the word is an inserted contrast at the end of a sentence; if so, set it off with a comma before and after (or with a single comma before if it ends the sentence) — here you need commas surrounding the parenthetical, so choose the option that provides the correct comma placement.'),
  ('cc9c85a7-78b0-5d08-9ece-3038f61af2a1', 2, null, '**Choice C is correct.** The sentence sets up a cause-and-effect relationship: because parts of the complex were physically inaccessible, LiDAR was the only way to reveal previously unknown aspects. "Hence," signals that what follows is a logical consequence of the prior clause, so it fits the passage’s meaning and flow.
**Choice A is incorrect.** "Similarly," signals a comparison to something else, but the passage requires a consequence, not a comparison, so it does not fit the context.
**Choice B is incorrect.** "Afterward," indicates a time sequence; the sentence needs to express causation rather than chronology, so this choice is inappropriate.
**Choice D is incorrect.** "Specifically," introduces a particular detail or example, but the sentence is presenting a causal result, not narrowing to a detail, so it is not the best transition.
**Faster approach:** Identify whether the sentence needs cause/effect, time sequence, comparison, or example; then choose a transition that matches that relationship (here, cause/effect → choose "hence/therefore").'),
  ('cde0df2d-d87c-527f-a1b8-fd7bc94c22d6', 2, null, '**Choice C is correct.** The two sentences set up a contrast between waste that becomes waste after consumer use (post-consumer) and waste that becomes waste before reaching the consumer (pre-consumer). "By contrast," signals that the second clause will present an opposing or differing case, so it logically connects the statements.
**Choice A is incorrect.** "For example," would introduce a specific instance that illustrates the first sentence, but the second sentence is not an example of post-consumer waste; it describes a different category.
**Choice B is incorrect.** "Specifically," narrows or clarifies a prior idea; here the second clause does not clarify the post-consumer example but instead contrasts a different type of waste.
**Choice D is incorrect.** "As a result," indicates cause and effect, which does not fit because the scraps did not result from the magazine being thrown away; they represent a different timing in the waste process.
**Faster approach:** Identify whether the second sentence shows contrast, cause, example, or clarification; pick the transition that matches (here, contrast = "By contrast").'),
  ('abd5300c-e2a9-50c7-962d-04a07da64274', 3, null, '**Choice D is correct.** The sentence before says that above the Kármán line an aircraft no longer remains aloft by lift; the next clause presents the contrasting means by which flight is sustained at that altitude (by velocity), so a contrastive transition is required. "Instead," explicitly signals that what follows contrasts with using lift, making the sentence coherent.
**Choice A is incorrect.** "Regardless," suggests disregard for a preceding condition and does not mark the necessary contrast between lift and velocity-based sustaining of flight.
**Choice B is incorrect.** "For instance," introduces an example; the sentence that follows is not merely an example but a contrast to the previous mechanism, so this transition is inappropriate.
**Choice C is incorrect.** "Granted," concedes a point; it doesn’t clearly set up the direct contrast needed between lift and orbital velocity.
**Faster approach:** Scan for the relationship signaled by the surrounding sentences (contrast, example, concession). Match that relationship to the transition word — here the contrast "no longer by lift" requires "Instead."'),
  ('caef1381-9ad0-50e8-ad3d-a12f7a730ae8', 2, null, '**Choice C is correct.** The note explicitly states, "It was founded in 1925," so restating that sentence most directly and effectively indicates the year the Schomburg Center was founded.
**Choice A is incorrect.** That sentence identifies for whom the center was named, not the year it was founded, so it does not answer the question about the founding year.
**Choice B is incorrect.** That sentence describes the type of institution (a research library) but gives no information about when it was founded.
**Choice D is incorrect.** That sentence provides the location (New York City) rather than the founding year, so it does not accomplish the student’s goal.
**Faster approach:** Scan the notes for the sentence containing a year; choose the option that repeats that sentence verbatim (it will directly answer a question about a date).'),
  ('49e3f748-ce15-5be7-9ba9-44abbc245d27', 1, null, '**Choice B is correct.** The notes state that brussels sprouts contain 80 mg of ascorbic acid per 100 g and apples contain 6 mg per 100 g; both are identified as containing ascorbic acid (vitamin C). Comparing the two amounts shows that brussels sprouts contain substantially more vitamin C than apples, so the statement that both contain vitamin C but brussels sprouts contain more accurately and effectively uses the relevant information.
**Choice A is incorrect.** Although it gives a precise value for brussels sprouts, it does not compare that value to the amount in apples and so does not accomplish the student’s goal of comparing vitamin C content.
**Choice C is incorrect.** That choice states categorical differences (vegetable vs fruit) but does not address vitamin C content or compare the two foods on that nutritional measure.
**Choice D is incorrect.** While true that humans must obtain ascorbic acid from food and that brussels sprouts are a source, this choice ignores apples and does not compare the vitamin C content of the two foods.
**Faster approach:** Compare the two given rates (mg per 100 g) directly; the larger numeric value identifies the food with more vitamin C (80 mg vs. 6 mg → brussels sprouts).'),
  ('02da3112-2b5d-5f8c-867d-5dd9693000cf', 0, null, '**Choice A is correct.** It emphasizes a clear similarity between the two films by stating that both include Merle Oberon; the notes explicitly identify her and state films in which she appeared, so this choice uses relevant information to highlight a shared feature of the films.
**Choice B is incorrect.** It points out a contrast in timing (one film early in her career and the other later) rather than emphasizing a similarity between the two films.
**Choice C is incorrect.** It contrasts the kinds of roles she had in the two films (uncredited vs. credited), so it highlights a difference rather than a similarity.
**Choice D is incorrect.** It only discusses her uncredited status early in her career and mentions one film, so it does not emphasize a similarity between the two films.
**Faster approach:** For questions asking for a similarity, scan choices for wording that describes a shared attribute (both, both include, both feature) and pick the option that explicitly states something both items have in common.')
on conflict (question_id) do update set correct_index = excluded.correct_index, correct_answers = excluded.correct_answers,
  explanation = excluded.explanation;

-- PeaklyTest May 2025 · Reading & Writing · Module 2
insert into public.tests (id, title, section, description, duration_min, position, published, form_title, module_no)
values ('20d6a66e-8bbc-5c22-8f14-0acdab378e79', 'PeaklyTest May 2025 · Reading & Writing · Module 2', 'Reading & Writing', 'Reading & Writing, Module 2 of the May 2025 PeaklyTest · 27 questions · 32 minutes', 32, 221, true, 'PeaklyTest May 2025 · Reading & Writing', 2)
on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, section = excluded.section, description = excluded.description,
  duration_min = excluded.duration_min, position = excluded.position, form_title = excluded.form_title, module_no = excluded.module_no;
insert into public.questions (id, test_id, prompt, options, position, passage) values
  ('10c8d9fd-aef1-5d19-8c82-f53ee6f1c1d8', '20d6a66e-8bbc-5c22-8f14-0acdab378e79', 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?', '["surpassed", "enhanced", "influenced", "protected"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The Apalachicola River delta in Florida is where the river flows into the Gulf of Mexico. As the river reaches the gulf, it forms smaller channels where sediments carried by the river are deposited and form many deltaic lobes, or wetland formations. The shape and size of the delta are _____ by the gulf’s tides, which shift sediments along the coast and reshape the deltaic lobes over time.'),
  ('edbe15f8-97d8-5517-b4d4-9bac1fa70670', '20d6a66e-8bbc-5c22-8f14-0acdab378e79', 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?', '["preceded", "summarized", "supplanted", "overlooked"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Lisa Linn Kanae is a celebrated contemporary Hawaiian author whose work is _____ by a rich literary history. From the traditional stories of the Kanaka Maoli, or Native Hawaiian people, to the works of writers such as David Malo, the literary heritage of Hawai‘i is distinctive and vibrant.'),
  ('01ac8ef6-76a2-5f5f-a9b6-f54987cc21a2', '20d6a66e-8bbc-5c22-8f14-0acdab378e79', 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?', '["expediency", "fecundity", "ubiquity", "heterogeneity"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Archaeologists have identified a plethora of beads fashioned from Tritia gibbosula shells in many Middle Stone Age (MSA) sites across western North Africa, including El Mnasra Cave, Morocco. In a 2021 paper, El Mehdi Sehasseh et al. attribute these artifacts'' _____ to the evolution and propagation of symbolic behavior (e.g., use of personal ornaments) in humans and the availability of T. gibbosula during the MSA.'),
  ('68169fdd-82da-546c-945e-a8f9ce8d2875', '20d6a66e-8bbc-5c22-8f14-0acdab378e79', 'Which choice best describes the function of the underlined portion in the text as a whole?', '["It specifies potential applications of the approach that Oliveira and colleagues used in their study.", "It offers commentary on the significance of the approach that Oliveira and colleagues used for their study.", "It emphasizes the importance of Oliveira and colleagues'' findings about the DNA of flora.", "It explains why the research methodology selected by Oliveira and colleagues is not widely used."]'::jsonb, 4, 'Svante Pääbo and other researchers studying the history of organisms have long utilized ancient DNA—DNA recovered from ancient organic material that has been preserved under natural conditions. However, Reyjane P. Oliveira and colleagues'' 2017 study of the evolutionary trajectory of the Malagasy grass genus Chasechloa instead relied on historical DNA—genomic data incidentally preserved in specimens that are housed in natural history collections—<u>thus capitalizing on the research potential</u> <u>offered by a vast but hitherto relatively underutilized source of insight into the biological past.</u>'),
  ('a08f7f92-0f82-5af4-a6df-00b469a2c5cd', '20d6a66e-8bbc-5c22-8f14-0acdab378e79', 'Which choice best describes the function of the underlined portion in the text as a whole?', '["It suggests that the researchers did not fully achieve a goal outlined earlier in the text.", "It describes the uncertain nature of a discovery by the researchers mentioned earlier in the text.", "It distinguishes between the two types of historical artifacts discussed earlier in the text.", "It refers to a challenging situation presented earlier in the text."]'::jsonb, 5, 'Established knowledge of prehistoric societies primarily stems from analysis of durable historical artifacts, including stone tools, but Mercedes Murillo-Barroso and team have contributed new insights with a unique analysis of plant-based items. Plant-based artifacts, including straw baskets, are seldom studied due to their perishable nature. <u>This relative scarcity</u> made it all the more exciting when the team examined remains of well-preserved organic artifacts discovered in a cave in Spain and determined that they dated even further back than previously thought.'),
  ('d570ff58-f839-5ed1-854a-d20c719bbf62', '20d6a66e-8bbc-5c22-8f14-0acdab378e79', 'Which choice best states the main purpose of the text?', '["It explores a subtle difference between Kean’s and Parisi’s respective objections to scientific research methods.", "It outlines an area of agreement between Parisi and Kean in how they think about the work of science.", "It describes Kean’s view that nonfiction narrative is an effective way for Parisi to share his accomplishments.", "It presents Kean’s claim that Parisi is too modest about his own achievements as a scientist."]'::jsonb, 6, 'In 2023 Nobel Prize-winning physicist Giorgio Parisi published In a Flight of Starlings, a collection of essays on scientific subjects. Acclaimed author Sam Kean, who uses the form of nonfiction narrative to explore scientific discoveries, is interested in the collection less for its aesthetic merit than for its focus on "the false starts...and mistakes that beset real scientific research every day." Kean argues that all too often, scientists omit this "human stuff" from written accounts of their research and thus ultimately "fail to explain how science really gets done."'),
  ('63be951b-5468-5617-b93e-b0127343f17e', '20d6a66e-8bbc-5c22-8f14-0acdab378e79', 'Which choice best describes the function of the underlined portion in the text as a whole?', '["It bolsters the idea that Camillo’s primary motivation to return home is his concern for the king of Sicily’s well-being in his absence.", "It suggests that Camillo feels compelled to persuade Polixenes that Camillo’s decision to leave the court is justified.", "It establishes Camillo’s hope that Polixenes will be comforted knowing that Camillo isn’t departing at his own discretion.", "It conveys Camillo’s recognition that one benefit his presence may afford the king of Sicily is merely speculative."]'::jsonb, 7, 'The following text is from William Shakespeare’s circa 1611 play The Winter’s Tale. Camillo has been away from his home in Sicily and serves in the court of Polixenes, the king of Bohemia. He has asked Polixenes for permission to return to Sicily.

POLIXENES: I pray thee, good Camillo, be no more importunate. ''Tis a sickness denying thee anything, a death to grant this.
CAMILLO: It is fifteen years since I saw my country. Though I have for the most part been aired abroad, I desire to lay my bones there. Besides, the penitent king, my master, hath sent for me, <u>to</u> <u>whose feeling sorrows I might be some allay— or I o''erween [presume] to think so</u>—which is another spur to my departure.'),
  ('003ef4fc-359f-5556-9370-d8c3d32d195a', '20d6a66e-8bbc-5c22-8f14-0acdab378e79', 'In the text, what does the speaker say that the artist believed about his sculpture?', '["It would be praised for its intricate details that he carefully chiseled.", "It would be carefully repaired when it inevitably begins to deteriorate.", "It would bring him enduring fame by withstanding time.", "Its beauty would be recognized as surpassing that of other images of Venus."]'::jsonb, 8, 'The following text is from Narciso Tondreau’s 1898 poem "Yesterday and To-Day" (translated by Agnes Blake Poor in 1918). The poem is about an old statue of the Greek goddess Venus, which now lies on the ground in a state of disrepair.

How long ago, by summer breezes fanned,
Here stood the newborn Venus, fresh and fair;
All palpitating from the master’s hand,
The last touch of his chisel lingering there.
"And surely this shall last!" he proudly thought;
"Fixed in immortal marble is my fame!"
Just here, where human hand has surely wrought,
Some crumbling letters may have spelled his name.'),
  ('ad2070a4-f97c-540d-a316-c6e20505c0b7', '20d6a66e-8bbc-5c22-8f14-0acdab378e79', 'Which choice best describes data in the table that support the city planner’s conclusion?', '["The enhancement of pollination was ranked higher for stakeholders than were the other four services.", "The prevention of soil erosion was ranked lower for stakeholders than it was for project leaders.", "The enhancement of pollination was ranked higher for the general public than it was for stakeholders.", "The enhancement of carbon sequestration was ranked higher for stakeholders than was the enhancement of pollination."]'::jsonb, 9, 'Esther Sanyé-Mengual, Kathrin Specht, and their team surveyed three groups of people in Bologna, Italy —leaders of urban agriculture projects, stakeholders in urban agriculture (e.g., food researchers and urban farming associations), and the general public—to compare their views about the extent to which urban agriculture contributes to 25 social or ecological services that the team identified. The researchers used these ratings to rank the services for each group, with a ranking of 1 indicating that a group perceives that urban agriculture benefits that service the most. Using only the rankings shown in the table, a city planner in Bologna who is promoting a new urban agriculture project concludes that advertisements aimed at stakeholders should emphasize the project’s benefit to the enhancement of pollination.

**Ranking of Environmental and Sociocultural benefits of Urban Agriculture (scale 1 to** **25; 1 = highest)**

| Social or ecological service | Project leaders | Stakeholders | General public |
| provision of medicinal plants | 22 | 21 | 5 |
| enhancement of pollination | 1 | 7 | 12 |
| enhancement of carbon sequestration | 17 | 22 | 21 |
| preservation of cultural knowledge and heritage | 8 | 15 | 13 |
| prevention of soil erosion | 13 | 11 | 23 |'),
  ('e422b785-f6cb-5d34-a720-55307cfa4255', '20d6a66e-8bbc-5c22-8f14-0acdab378e79', 'Which finding, if true, would most directly support the researchers'' assertion?', '["IGF1 has been isolated in genetic material from fossils more than 20,000 years old of the red wolf (Canis rufus) and certain other species related to dogs.", "An additional mutation related to IGF1 affects the development of characteristics other than body size in smaller dog breeds like bulldogs.", "The mutation related to IGF1 influences body size and is found in 53,000-year-old genetic material from fossils of Siberian wolves (Canis lupus campestris), an ancestor of dogs.", "One variant of IGF1 is found only in smaller dog breeds like bulldogs and appears to have emerged no more than 20,000 years ago."]'::jsonb, 10, 'In an international collaboration, Elaine Ostrander, Alan K. Outram, and other researchers probed the evolutionary history of size variation in modern dogs. Scientific consensus held that early dogs had large body mass and that a genetic driver of smaller size in some breeds (e.g., bulldogs) developed only within the last 20,000 years as a result of selective breeding for characteristics favored by humans. Ostrander et al. assert that this explanation is flawed, having discovered that a mutation responsible for variants of IGF1, a gene found in many mammals that regulates production of insulin-like growth factor 1, is ubiquitous in domestic dog breeds.'),
  ('78b5c43a-39e2-57bd-a677-c59108a7ea65', '20d6a66e-8bbc-5c22-8f14-0acdab378e79', 'Which finding, if true, would most directly challenge the second research team’s conclusion?', '["The study by Kozak and Keoleian had a lower detail rating than the study by Oguchi and colleagues did.", "The study by Kozak and Keoleian had a lower traceability rating than the study by Oguchi and colleagues did.", "The study by Kozak and Keoleian had a high detail rating and a high traceability rating.", "The study by Oguchi and colleagues had a low detail rating and a low traceability rating."]'::jsonb, 11, 'Callie W. Babbitt, Hema Madaka, and colleagues assembled a database of materials used in consumer electronics by studying products in the lab and by gathering data from similar product studies. The team gave each of these studies a rating for level of detail (with a higher rating for reported data with more detail) and for level of traceability (with a higher rating for clearer descriptions of procedures). Based on these ratings, a second research team concluded that a study by Greg L. Kozak and Gregory A. Keoleian provided more specificity in its data than a study by Oguchi Masahiro and colleagues did.'),
  ('07e889b0-eb75-5d93-a490-8d43075cddf2', '20d6a66e-8bbc-5c22-8f14-0acdab378e79', 'Which finding, if true, would most directly support the researchers'' conclusion?', '["The effect of soil temperature on the growth of vegetation and on the rate of heterotrophic respiration is stronger during the period of seasonal snow cover than during the period without snow cover.", "Relatively early snow cover reduces the amount of soil moisture available for the growth of plant species such as R. arcticus and lowers the rate of heterotrophic respiration.", "Seasonal snow cover tends to persist longer in areas of relatively low vegetation growth and high heterotrophic respiration than in areas of relatively high vegetation growth and low heterotrophic respiration.", "The soil insulation provided by snow cover enables heterotrophic respiration to continue during a period in which plant species such as R. arcticus are typically not growing."]'::jsonb, 12, 'Soil thawing in Alaska has been accelerating as a result of climate changes, potentially enabling increased carbon dioxide (CO₂) absorption through greater productivity of arctic bramble (Rubus arcticus) plants and other vegetation, but also potentially enabling increased CO₂ output through greater heterotrophic respiration (CO₂ generated by the activity of soil microorganisms). Hydrologist Yonghong Yi and her colleagues developed a model incorporating numerous inputs—years of soil temperature and snow cover data among them—to evaluate the effects of climate changes on the CO₂ balance in Alaska, concluding that net CO₂ is likely to increase if seasonal snow cover arrives earlier relative to the onset of soil surface freezing.'),
  ('80cc0d0e-946e-5352-9c65-fc74a7b697a2', '20d6a66e-8bbc-5c22-8f14-0acdab378e79', 'Which choice most logically completes the text?', '["Chinese immigrants who emigrated to New York City many years ago are more likely to speak several Chinese languages than are more recent Chinese immigrants.", "people who emigrate from north China tend to settle in Queens, while people who emigrate from south China tend to settle in Manhattan.", "taken together, there are more Cantonese and Fuzhounese speakers among Chinese immigrants in New York City than there are Mandarin speakers.", "Chinese immigrants regularly change their residences between Queens and Manhattan after they emigrate, rather than staying in one borough."]'::jsonb, 13, 'Over 600 languages are spoken in New York City in addition to English—one can find Bambara spoken in the neighborhood of Kingsbridge, or Ghale in Jackson Heights. Most speakers of Chinese languages reside in the neighborhood of Flushing (part of New York City’s borough of Queens), where the dominant Chinese language is Mandarin, and in Chinatown, in the borough of Manhattan, where the dominant Chinese languages are Cantonese and Fuzhounese. Mandarin is widely spoken in north China, while Cantonese and Fuzhounese are widely spoken in south China. It can therefore be inferred that _____'),
  ('36c79927-67be-5999-b6e0-6b7dd1f24941', '20d6a66e-8bbc-5c22-8f14-0acdab378e79', 'Which choice most logically completes the text?', '["Black Swan Green is not as worthy of praise as it might have been if it had no basis in fact.", "critics disagree about whether Black Swan Green shows greater originality than works without autobiographical elements.", "the real-world counterparts of other characters in Black Swan Green are hard to identify.", "Mitchell should not have claimed that Black Swan Green is based on real events."]'::jsonb, 14, 'David Mitchell’s 2006 novel Black Swan Green contains elements drawn from Mitchell’s own life—there are many parallels between the experiences of the novel’s protagonist, Jason Taylor, and those of Mitchell—and as a result Black Swan Green is regularly described as an autobiographical novel. This characterization can be useful, but it also presents drawbacks in terms of how the work is perceived, given the high value placed on creativity and imagination in artistic endeavors. Readers who take this characterization to mean that Mitchell merely fictionalized actual events may conclude that _____'),
  ('403842a7-604a-583b-8417-8535ca614fe1', '20d6a66e-8bbc-5c22-8f14-0acdab378e79', 'Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?', '["periods. The", "periods; the", "periods: the", "periods, the"]'::jsonb, 15, 'Since the nineteenth century, Egyptologists have commonly divided ancient Egyptian history into three primary _____ Old Kingdom (2700—2200 BCE), the Middle Kingdom (2050—1800 BCE), and the New Kingdom (1550—1100 BCE). Some historians, however, criticize the names of these periods for revealing more about the culture of the mainly European Egyptologists than that of ancient Egypt itself.'),
  ('382f2f25-b8c8-578c-bb39-2075d828fe94', '20d6a66e-8bbc-5c22-8f14-0acdab378e79', 'Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?', '["song", "song,", "song:", "song;"]'::jsonb, 16, 'If you had turned on the radio in the fall of 1964, chances are you would have heard the hit _____ "Little Marie" by Chuck Berry. The song spent nine straight weeks on the Billboard Hot 100 chart that year.'),
  ('2e8da84b-0c9c-5f73-b4b5-401015838bae', '20d6a66e-8bbc-5c22-8f14-0acdab378e79', 'Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?', '["are", "was", "have been", "were"]'::jsonb, 17, 'The allocation of a new country dialing prefix (+380) to route incoming international calls _____ among the infrastructural changes necessitated by Ukraine’s 1991 split from the Soviet Union.'),
  ('b41cc59a-00da-54c7-a045-e4910f9812eb', '20d6a66e-8bbc-5c22-8f14-0acdab378e79', 'Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?', '["meteoroids—their", "meteoroids, their", "meteoroids; their", "meteoroids. Their"]'::jsonb, 18, 'The Lyrid meteor shower’s brightness typically surpasses that of the Puppid-Velid meteor shower due to the interplay of three main factors— namely, the size and composition of the _____ velocity upon entering Earth’s mesosphere, with higher-velocity meteoroids releasing more energy; and atmospheric fluctuations, such as changes in humidity.'),
  ('3ffa9e6e-8f83-54ee-a145-5f6ed2fe0772', '20d6a66e-8bbc-5c22-8f14-0acdab378e79', 'Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?', '["influence", "influences", "is influencing", "has influenced"]'::jsonb, 19, 'In Hafez’s Divān, the writer muses on love, faith, and protest. So impactful is this particular work by Hafez, a Persian lyric poet whose authorial perspective was at once mystical and critical, that its ruminations on fourteenth-century Persian life _____ Iranian society hundreds of years later.'),
  ('f16e124b-c617-58d4-96ff-d240c9bf3180', '20d6a66e-8bbc-5c22-8f14-0acdab378e79', 'Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?', '["photographer Mária Švarbová,", "photographer Mária Švarbová", "photographer, Mária Švarbová", "photographer, Mária Švarbová,"]'::jsonb, 20, 'In 2018, the Contessa Gallery in Miami, Florida, exhibited the work of Slovak _____ who aptly describes her photos as "minimalistic but also futuristic."'),
  ('2a61500a-0b40-508e-8e86-5f2c5042c433', '20d6a66e-8bbc-5c22-8f14-0acdab378e79', 'Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?', '["was prioritizing", "had prioritized", "prioritizing", "prioritized"]'::jsonb, 21, 'Instead of sleeping on soft pillows, ancient Egyptians slept on hard, elevated headrests, their design _____ airflow, protection from insects, and hairstyle maintenance over comfort.'),
  ('3f08da8b-fffa-5f76-aaac-fc4c8a1b987c', '20d6a66e-8bbc-5c22-8f14-0acdab378e79', 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?', '["In addition,", "However,", "For instance,", "In comparison,"]'::jsonb, 22, 'French mathematician Michel Talagrand has shown that over large numbers of trials, variables that appear to be independent and random are in fact extremely likely to cluster around an average. _____ according to Talagrand’s research, if you flip a coin 1,000 times, there is a 99.7% probability that the coin will land heads side up between 450 and 550 times.'),
  ('2e170a64-bcaf-5cd7-9d5b-e88cb502d258', '20d6a66e-8bbc-5c22-8f14-0acdab378e79', 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?', '["afterward,", "in conclusion,", "for example,", "by contrast,"]'::jsonb, 23, 'Topographic maps show the elevation of landforms above sea level. Bathymetric maps, _____ show the elevation of landforms below the sea, providing valuable information to marine geophysicists like Claudia Flores, who studies seismic data from the northeastern Caribbean.'),
  ('219cc443-68e1-50d3-8798-1188ea9d704a', '20d6a66e-8bbc-5c22-8f14-0acdab378e79', 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?', '["In addition,", "Specifically,", "Thus,", "By comparison,"]'::jsonb, 24, 'Edge modeling is one process digital artists employ while developing visuals for video games. _____ digital artists use edge modeling to piece together separate shapes to create 3D objects for games.'),
  ('92d449c0-68c7-5fbb-ad5d-a5517873737d', '20d6a66e-8bbc-5c22-8f14-0acdab378e79', 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?', '["For example,", "Eventually,", "In other words,", "Concurrently,"]'::jsonb, 25, 'In the Southeast Asian country of Indonesia, the percentage of the population living in cities rose from 53.3% to 56.6% between 2015 and 2020. _____ urbanization rates climbed across Southeast Asia as a whole, rising 2.8 percentage points in that five-year period.'),
  ('e8fd0452-e0a8-518f-9bff-88f387a576df', '20d6a66e-8bbc-5c22-8f14-0acdab378e79', 'The student wants to make a generalization about how word order is employed across languages. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?', '["Word order is one of the structural features by which linguistic typologists classify and compare the world’s languages.", "Linguistic typologists analyze and compare how word order is employed across languages.", "The way in which subjects, verbs, and objects are typically arranged in a sentence is also called word order.", "The majority of languages place the subject of a sentence before the verb and object."]'::jsonb, 26, 'While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:

Linguistic typologists classify and compare languages according to their structural features. One such structure is word order, or the way subjects (S), verbs (V), and objects (O) are typically arranged in a sentence.
English employs an SVO word order (e.g., Cows eat grass).
Bengali employs an SOV word order (e.g., Cows grass eat).
Filipino employs a VSO word order (e.g., Eat cows grass).
87% of the world’s languages are classified as either SVO or SOV.'),
  ('02ed2ec2-c743-58e7-9162-3826b0f0df91', '20d6a66e-8bbc-5c22-8f14-0acdab378e79', 'The student wants to emphasize the significance of a similarity between two of the kits. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?', '["CTAB 2X and Pplant both had a DNA yield above 10 and a purity ratio above 1.4.", "Due to their insufficient yield or purity, CTAB 2X and Pplant were deemed by the researchers to be less effective than Pbact.", "With the ideal purity ratio being 1.8, CTAB 2X and Pbact were equal in purity, according to the researchers.", "Compared to CTAB 2X, which had a DNA yield of 2,134 ng/μL, both Pbact and Pplant had insufficient yields; Pplant, in particular, was ineffective due to its low yield."]'::jsonb, 27, 'While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:
Producing the nutrient-rich cyanobacterium L. maxima at industrial scale requires high-quality samples of L. maxima DNA.
Yirlis Yadeth Pineda-Rodriguez and a team of researchers at the University of Córdoba, Colombia, evaluated the quantity and purity of L. maxima DNA extracted using three different DNA extraction kits.
CTAB 2X (kit 1) had a DNA yield of 2,134 nanograms per microliter (ng/μL) and a purity ratio of 2.2. Pbact (kit 2) had a DNA yield of 157 ng/μL and a purity ratio of 1.6.
Pplant (kit 3) had a DNA yield of 12.5 ng/μL and a purity ratio of 1.5.
According to the researchers, Pbact was the most effective because it was the only one with both a sufficiently high yield and a purity rate close to the ideal of 1.8.')
on conflict (id) do update set test_id = excluded.test_id, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options,
  position = excluded.position, passage = excluded.passage;
insert into public.question_keys (question_id, correct_index, correct_answers, explanation) values
  ('10c8d9fd-aef1-5d19-8c82-f53ee6f1c1d8', 2, null, '**Choice C is correct.** The sentence describes how the gulf’s tides alter the delta by shifting sediments and reshaping lobes; the verb "influenced" accurately conveys that the tides have an effect on the shape and size. It is precise and neutral, matching the explanatory tone of the passage.
**Choice A is incorrect.** "Surpassed" means to exceed or go beyond, which does not make sense for how tides act on a geographic feature.
**Choice B is incorrect.** "Enhanced" suggests improvement or making better; the passage describes neutral or altering effects (shifting and reshaping), not improvement.
**Choice D is incorrect.** "Protected" means to guard or preserve, which contradicts the passage’s statement that tides shift sediments and reshape lobes over time.
**Faster approach:** Substitute each choice into the sentence and read it with the following clause; eliminate any option that creates a semantic contradiction with "which shift sediments along the coast and reshape the deltaic lobes over time." This quickly identifies "influenced" as the only logically consistent choice.'),
  ('edbe15f8-97d8-5517-b4d4-9bac1fa70670', 0, null, '**Choice A is correct.** The sentence sets up a relationship between Kanae’s work and the longstanding literary traditions of Hawai‘i; saying her work "is preceded by a rich literary history" clearly conveys that the literary heritage came before and provides context for her writing. The following sentence, which lists traditional stories and earlier writers, supports this temporal and contextual relationship.
**Choice B is incorrect.** "Summarized by" would mean Kanae’s work condenses the literary history, which contradicts the passage: the history is presented as antecedent background, not as content summarized by her work.
**Choice C is incorrect.** "Supplanted by" means replaced by; nothing in the passage implies that Kanae’s work replaces the rich literary history—if anything, the passage emphasizes continuity with that past.
**Choice D is incorrect.** "Overlooked by" would imply the literary history ignores her work, which is the opposite of the passage’s intent that her work is connected to and emerges from that heritage.
**Faster approach:** Look for a verb phrase that expresses a temporal or causal relationship with the preceding heritage—eliminate words implying replacement, summarization, or ignoring, leaving the option that indicates the history comes before and informs the work.'),
  ('01ac8ef6-76a2-5f5f-a9b6-f54987cc21a2', 2, null, '**Choice C is correct.** The sentence attributes a feature of the shell beads to two factors: the
evolution/propagation of symbolic behavior (use of personal ornaments) and the availability of T. gibbosula during the MSA. The noun that fits both causes is a term meaning that the artifacts were widespread or commonly found—"ubiquity." Saying the beads'' ubiquity is due to symbolic practices and ready access to the shells is precise and logical.
**Choice A is incorrect.** "Expediency" means practicality or convenience; it does not describe a property of the artifacts that would be explained by both symbolic behavior and shell availability.
**Choice B is incorrect.** "Fecundity" relates to fertility or productivity; it is not an appropriate descriptor for artifacts in this context.
**Choice D is incorrect.** "Heterogeneity" means diversity or variety; attributing diversity to both symbolic behavior and shell availability is less direct than asserting their widespread occurrence.
**Faster approach:** Look for a noun that both proposed causes would plausibly explain (e.g.,
commonness/occurrence); eliminate options that denote unrelated qualities (fertility, convenience, diversity).'),
  ('68169fdd-82da-546c-945e-a8f9ce8d2875', 1, null, '**Choice B is correct.** The underlined phrase characterizes Oliveira and colleagues'' use of historical DNA as a beneficial opportunity, calling it a way of "capitalizing on the research potential" of an extensive but underused source. That is commentary on the significance and value of their approach within the passage’s contrast between ancient and historical DNA.
**Choice A is incorrect.** The phrase does not list specific applications or uses of the approach; it makes a general evaluative statement about its research potential rather than detailing particular applications.
**Choice C is incorrect.** The clause does not report specific findings about plant DNA; it evaluates the methodological resource (historical DNA) rather than describing results about flora.
**Choice D is incorrect.** The phrase does not explain why the methodology is unpopular; instead it notes that the source has been "relatively underutilized" while emphasizing its potential, not giving reasons for limited use.
**Faster approach:** Look for wording that signals value (words like "capitalizing," "potential," "underutilized") to identify a statement as commentary on significance rather than as a description of applications, findings, or causes.'),
  ('a08f7f92-0f82-5af4-a6df-00b469a2c5cd', 3, null, '**Choice D is correct.** The phrase "This relative scarcity" refers back to the earlier statement that plant-based artifacts are "seldom studied due to their perishable nature," and the sentence uses that scarcity to set up why the discovery was especially noteworthy. In other words, the phrase points to a previously mentioned difficulty (the scarcity of plant-based items) and links it to the significance of the find, so it functions as a reference to a challenging situation presented earlier.
**Choice A is incorrect.** The phrase does not imply researchers failed to achieve a goal; it simply notes a limitation of the evidence, not an unmet objective.
**Choice B is incorrect.** The phrase does not describe uncertainty about the discovery; it describes the lack (scarcity) of such artifacts, not ambiguity about the find itself.
**Choice C is incorrect.** Although the passage mentions two types of artifacts, the phrase does not serve to distinguish them; it specifically refers to the scarcity of plant-based artifacts, not a comparison of categories.
**Faster approach:** Identify the noun or idea the pronoun/phrase refers to and ask whether that referent is presented as a problem, a contrast, or uncertainty; if it points to a limitation mentioned earlier, choose the option that calls it a challenging situation.'),
  ('d570ff58-f839-5ed1-854a-d20c719bbf62', 1, null, '**Choice B is correct.** The passage reports that Parisi’s collection emphasizes “the false starts...and mistakes” of research and that Kean appreciates that focus; Kean’s argument that scientists omit the “human stuff” supports the idea that both authors value exposing how science is actually done. The passage therefore highlights a shared perspective on the nature of scientific work.
**Choice A is incorrect.** The passage does not present a contrast or subtle difference between Parisi’s and Kean’s objections; it emphasizes agreement about the importance of showing mistakes in science.
**Choice C is incorrect.** The passage says Kean is interested in Parisi’s focus on false starts, not that nonfiction narrative is primarily an effective way for Parisi to share accomplishments.
**Choice D is incorrect.** Nothing in the passage claims that Parisi is too modest about his achievements; the focus is on how Parisi depicts scientific process, not on his personal modesty.
**Faster approach:** Identify the passage’s main claim about the relationship between the two authors (agreement vs. contrast) and eliminate choices that assert personal attributes or unmentioned evaluations—this quickly isolates the answer.'),
  ('63be951b-5468-5617-b93e-b0127343f17e', 3, null, '**Choice D is correct.** The underlined clause—“to whose feeling sorrows I might be some allay—or I o''erween [presume] to think so”—frames the comfort Camillo might provide as tentative. Words like "might" and the self-correction "or I o''erween" explicitly mark the benefit as speculative rather than certain, so the function is to acknowledge that this possible advantage to the Sicilian king is merely conjectural.
**Choice A is incorrect.** The clause does not bolster a primary motivation; instead, it qualifies the benefit and expresses doubt, so it does not strengthen the claim that concern for the king is his chief reason.
**Choice B is incorrect.** The line is not mainly an attempt to persuade Polixenes; it reports Camillo’s own tentative judgment about comforting his master, not an argument directed at Polixenes.
**Choice C is incorrect.** The clause does not communicate hope that Polixenes will be comforted by Camillo’s motive; it simply hedges the claim that Camillo would actually comfort the Sicilian king, signaling uncertainty rather than reassurance to Polixenes.
**Faster approach:** Spot hedging words (e.g., "might," "perhaps," "or I o''erween")—they indicate speculation or doubt, so choose the answer that identifies the statement as tentative or qualified.'),
  ('003ef4fc-359f-5556-9370-d8c3d32d195a', 2, null, '**Choice C is correct.** The speaker quotes the artist: “And surely this shall last!” and “Fixed in immortal marble is my fame!” Those lines show the artist believed the sculpture would endure over time and that this permanence would secure his lasting reputation — in other words, enduring fame by withstanding time.
**Choice A is incorrect.** Although the passage mentions the chisel and the last touch, it does not say the artist expected praise specifically for intricate details; the emphasis is on permanence and fame, not on praise for detail.
**Choice B is incorrect.** The artist’s thought is about the work lasting, not about future repairs; there is no suggestion he expected it to be repaired when it deteriorated.
**Choice D is incorrect.** The lines do not claim the artist believed his Venus surpassed other images of Venus in beauty; they focus on immortality and fame rather than comparative superiority.
**Faster approach:** Look for direct quoted lines expressing the artist’s belief and paraphrase them; quoted statements usually state the intended meaning directly, so matching wording to choices quickly identifies the correct answer.'),
  ('ad2070a4-f97c-540d-a316-c6e20505c0b7', 0, null, '**Choice A is correct.** For stakeholders the enhancement of pollination has a rank of $7$, while the other four listed services (provision of medicinal plants $= 21$, enhancement of carbon sequestration $= 22$, preservation of cultural knowledge and heritage $= 15$, prevention of soil erosion $= 11$) all have larger rank numbers. Because a smaller rank number indicates a higher perceived benefit, pollination (7) is ranked higher by stakeholders than each of the other four services.
**Choice B is incorrect.** Prevention of soil erosion is ranked $11$ for stakeholders and $13$ for project leaders, so stakeholders ranked it more highly (a smaller number), not lower.
**Choice C is incorrect.** The general public ranked pollination $12$ and stakeholders $7$, so the public ranked it lower (less important) than stakeholders.
**Choice D is incorrect.** Stakeholders ranked carbon sequestration $22$ and pollination $7$, so carbon sequestration was ranked much lower (less important), not higher, than pollination.
**Faster approach:** Directly compare the numeric ranks in the stakeholders column—because smaller numbers mean higher importance, the smallest number in that column among the five services (7) identifies the top-ranked service immediately.'),
  ('e422b785-f6cb-5d34-a720-55307cfa4255', 2, null, '**Choice C is correct.** It gives the most direct evidence that the mutation affecting body size existed well before 20,000 years ago by identifying the same mutation in $53,000$-year-old fossils of a wolf ancestor of dogs. That both ties the mutation to body-size effects and places it earlier than the proposed 20,000-year timeframe, directly undercutting the idea that the mutation arose only through recent selective breeding.
**Choice A is incorrect.** Finding IGF1 in related species older than 20,000 years does not show the specific mutation that affects size existed in the dog lineage or that it influenced size.
**Choice B is incorrect.** A mutation affecting other traits does not address when the size-related mutation arose or whether it was ubiquitous in breeds.
**Choice D is incorrect.** If a variant is found only in smaller breeds and emerged no more than 20,000 years ago, that supports the conventional explanation rather than the researchers'' claim.
**Faster approach:** For evidence questions, eliminate choices that lack a direct link among the key elements (the specific mutation, its effect on size, and a date earlier than 20,000 years); choose the option that explicitly connects all three.'),
  ('78b5c43a-39e2-57bd-a677-c59108a7ea65', 0, null, '**Choice A is correct.** The research team’s conclusion rests on the two ratings (detail and traceability) that determine how specific a study’s data are. If Kozak and Keoleian actually had a lower detail rating than Oguchi and colleagues, that directly contradicts the claim that Kozak’s study provided more specificity, because one of the central measures of specificity favors Oguchi.
**Choice B is incorrect.** A lower traceability rating for Kozak would also weaken the conclusion, but traceability alone is less directly tied to the specificity of the data than the level-of-detail rating; the most direct challenge is about the detail rating.
**Choice C is incorrect.** High detail and high traceability for Kozak would support—not challenge—the conclusion that Kozak’s study provided more specificity.
**Choice D is incorrect.** Low detail and low traceability for Oguchi would strengthen the second team’s conclusion, not challenge it.
**Faster approach:** Identify which measured attribute(s) the conclusion depends on (here, detail and traceability) and pick the choice that reverses those measures for the favored study.'),
  ('07e889b0-eb75-5d93-a490-8d43075cddf2', 3, null, '**Choice D is correct.** The researchers conclude that net CO₂ is likely to increase when seasonal snow cover arrives earlier than soil freezing; choice D provides the direct mechanism that would produce that result: snow insulates the soil so that heterotrophic respiration (microbial CO₂ release) continues during a time when plants such as Rubus arcticus are not growing and therefore not taking up CO₂. That increased winter respiration without compensating plant uptake would raise net CO₂.
**Choice A is incorrect.** It asserts only that temperature effects are stronger under snow cover but does not explain how earlier snow arrival would cause net CO₂ to increase.
**Choice B is incorrect.** It predicts reduced soil moisture and lowered respiration and plant growth with early snow, which would not support an increase in net CO₂.
**Choice C is incorrect.** It describes a correlation between snow persistence and vegetation/respiration levels but does not offer the causal mechanism linking earlier snow arrival to increased net CO₂.
**Faster approach:** Look for an answer that gives a direct causal mechanism connecting earlier snow to greater microbial CO₂ release while plants are inactive — the choice describing insulation-driven winter respiration is the quickest match.'),
  ('80cc0d0e-946e-5352-9c65-fc74a7b697a2', 1, null, '**Choice B is correct.** The passage states that Mandarin is dominant in Flushing (Queens) and that Mandarin is widely spoken in north China; it also states that Cantonese and Fuzhounese are dominant in Manhattan’s Chinatown and are widely spoken in south China. The most direct inference is that immigrants from north China tend to cluster where Mandarin is dominant (Queens) and immigrants from south China tend to cluster where Cantonese and Fuzhounese are dominant (Manhattan).
**Choice A is incorrect.** The passage gives no information about how long immigrants have been in New York or whether length of residence affects the number of Chinese languages an individual speaks.
**Choice C is incorrect.** No overall counts or comparisons of numbers of speakers are provided, so one cannot conclude that Cantonese+Fuzhounese speakers outnumber Mandarin speakers.
**Choice D is incorrect.** The passage describes where different language groups reside, but it does not mention frequent moves between boroughs.
**Faster approach:** Map the language-to-place statements to origin regions; match "north/south China → Mandarin/Cantonese+Fuzhounese → Queens/Manhattan" to get the inference quickly.'),
  ('36c79927-67be-5999-b6e0-6b7dd1f24941', 0, null, '**Choice A is correct.** The passage says labeling the novel autobiographical “presents drawbacks” because creativity and imagination are highly valued; if readers think the book merely fictionalizes real events, they are likely to see it as less original and therefore less praiseworthy than a purely imaginative work. This interpretation directly supports choice A.
**Choice B is incorrect.** The passage does not claim critics disagree about originality; it describes a likely reader reaction, not a debate among critics.
**Choice C is incorrect.** The passage makes no mention of difficulty identifying real-world counterparts to characters; that is unrelated to the concern about perceived creativity.
**Choice D is incorrect.** The passage criticizes the effects of the autobiographical label on perception, not Mitchell’s decision to describe his sources; it does not assert he should not have made such a claim.
**Faster approach:** Identify the problem the passage highlights (devaluation of creativity) and choose the answer that expresses that direct consequence—eliminate choices about critics, identification of characters, or author intent.'),
  ('403842a7-604a-583b-8417-8535ca614fe1', 2, null, '**Choice C is correct.** A colon is used to introduce a list or series that elaborates what precedes it. The phrase "three primary" is followed by the specific items (Old Kingdom, the Middle Kingdom, and the New Kingdom), so a colon correctly connects the general statement to the list that follows: "three primary periods: Old Kingdom..."
**Choice A is incorrect.** A period would end the sentence; starting a new sentence with "The Old Kingdom..." would create a choppy sentence structure and omit the clear, conventional introduction of the list.
**Choice B is incorrect.** A semicolon links independent clauses; here the second part is not an independent clause but a list of items, so a semicolon is inappropriate.
**Choice D is incorrect.** A comma cannot properly introduce a full list of items in this construction; it would create a comma splice or a run-on connection between the general phrase and the list.
**Faster approach:** Use a colon when what follows is a list or elaboration of what precedes—if the sentence proceeds to name items, choose a colon.'),
  ('382f2f25-b8c8-578c-bb39-2075d828fe94', 0, null, '**Choice A is correct.** The phrase "the hit song ''Little Marie'' by Chuck Berry" uses the noun "song" directly to modify the title; no intervening punctuation is required. The sentence reads naturally and follows Standard English conventions for a noun followed immediately by the title it names.
**Choice B is incorrect.** A comma after "song" would create an unnecessary pause and separate the noun from the title it modifies, which is not standard when a title directly follows its noun.
**Choice C is incorrect.** A colon would be incorrect because a colon typically introduces a list, explanation, or quotation that is syntactically independent; here the title functions as a noun phrase modifier and should not be introduced by a colon.
**Choice D is incorrect.** A semicolon is used to link independent clauses or complex list items, neither of which applies here; inserting a semicolon between "song" and the title is grammatically wrong.
**Faster approach:** Quickly read the phrase with and without punctuation—if the title is simply naming the noun just before it ("song ''Little Marie''"), no punctuation is needed; eliminate choices that add unnecessary marks.'),
  ('2e8da84b-0c9c-5f73-b4b5-401015838bae', 1, null, '**Choice B is correct.** The subject of the sentence is the singular noun phrase "The allocation of a new country dialing prefix...," so it requires a singular past-tense verb to match the past timing set by "necessitated by Ukraine’s 1991 split." "Was" is singular and past, so it correctly completes the sentence: "The allocation... was among the infrastructural changes necessitated by Ukraine’s 1991 split..."
**Choice A is incorrect.** "Are" is plural present tense and does not agree with the singular subject or the past time frame indicated by "necessitated."
**Choice C is incorrect.** "Have been" is plural present perfect and conflicts with the singular subject and the past context.
**Choice D is incorrect.** "Were" is plural past tense; although it matches the past time frame, it disagrees in number with the singular subject.
**Faster approach:** Identify the subject (singular "allocation") and the time frame (past "necessitated by...1991"); choose a singular past-tense verb ("was").'),
  ('b41cc59a-00da-54c7-a045-e4910f9812eb', 2, null, '**Choice C is correct.** The sentence names three main factors, and the middle item contains its own internal comma clause ("with higher-velocity meteoroids releasing more energy"). When items in a list contain internal commas, semicolons should be used to separate the main items so the list remains clear and parallel: "the size and composition of the meteoroids; their velocity upon entering Earth’s mesosphere, with higher-velocity meteoroids releasing more energy; and atmospheric fluctuations..."
**Choice A is incorrect.** An em dash would create an abrupt break and is inconsistent with a three-item list; it also conflicts with the earlier dash and disrupts parallelism.
**Choice B is incorrect.** A comma between the first two items makes the list ambiguous because the second item contains its own comma; mixed comma/semicolon separation reduces clarity.
**Choice D is incorrect.** A period would produce a sentence fragment ("Their velocity..."), which is ungrammatical here.
**Faster approach:** Identify the list introduced by "namely." If any listed item contains internal commas, separate the main items with semicolons.'),
  ('3ffa9e6e-8f83-54ee-a145-5f6ed2fe0772', 0, null, '**Choice A is correct.** The subject ''its ruminations'' is plural, so it requires a plural verb; ''influence'' is the present-tense plural form that correctly agrees with that subject and appropriately conveys a continuing, long-term effect described by ''hundreds of years later.''
**Choice B is incorrect.** ''Influences'' is third-person singular and does not agree with the plural subject ''ruminations.''
**Choice C is incorrect.** ''Is influencing'' uses the singular auxiliary ''is'' (a plural subject would require ''are influencing'') and the progressive aspect suggests an action happening right now rather than a historical, lasting effect.
**Choice D is incorrect.** ''Has influenced'' uses the singular auxiliary ''has''; with the plural subject it would need ''have influenced,'' so the form is ungrammatical as written.
**Faster approach:** Identify whether the subject is singular or plural, eliminate verb choices that do not match in number, and check auxiliaries/aspect against time markers (like ''later'') to select the form fitting both agreement and temporal meaning.'),
  ('f16e124b-c617-58d4-96ff-d240c9bf3180', 0, null, '**Choice A is correct.** The clause "who aptly describes her photos as ''minimalistic but also futuristic.''" is a nonrestrictive relative clause that adds information about the named photographer and therefore must be set off with a comma before "who." The name belongs to the noun phrase "Slovak photographer Mária Švarbová" and should remain attached to that phrase, so the comma belongs after the name as shown in A.
**Choice B is incorrect.** It omits the comma before "who," failing to mark the nonrestrictive clause and making the sentence ungrammatical.
**Choice C is incorrect.** It inserts a comma before the name but fails to close the appositive, producing unbalanced punctuation.
**Choice D is incorrect.** It unnecessarily separates the title from the name by putting a comma before "Mária Švarbová." The preferred construction keeps the name attached to the noun phrase and uses a single comma to introduce the nonrestrictive "who" clause.
**Faster approach:** Ask whether the "who" clause is essential; if not, you need a comma immediately before "who." Then pick the option that places that comma after the person’s name without breaking the noun phrase.'),
  ('2a61500a-0b40-508e-8e86-5f2c5042c433', 2, null, '**Choice C is correct.** The sentence links two related ideas with a noun phrase plus a participial phrase: "ancient Egyptians slept on hard, elevated headrests, their design prioritizing airflow..." Using the present participle "prioritizing" creates a nonclausal modifier that appropriately describes the headrests without forming a second independent clause.
**Choice A is incorrect.** "was prioritizing" would create a separate finite verb phrase and, when attached with only a comma, results in a comma splice (two independent clauses joined incorrectly).
**Choice B is incorrect.** "had prioritized" is a past-perfect finite verb and likewise would form an independent clause, producing a comma splice and an inappropriate tense for this descriptive context.
**Choice D is incorrect.** "prioritized" is a simple past finite verb and would make a second independent clause; joining it to the preceding clause with only a comma is grammatically incorrect (comma splice).
**Faster approach:** Watch for comma splices — after a comma you need either a nonfinite modifier (e.g., a participle) or a coordinating conjunction/semicolon; pick the participle when the phrase should be a descriptive modifier.'),
  ('3f08da8b-fffa-5f76-aaac-fc4c8a1b987c', 2, null, '**Choice C is correct.** The sentence that follows gives a specific example illustrating Talagrand’s general claim about clustering around an average, so a signal phrase like “For instance,” correctly introduces that example and creates a logical flow.
**Choice A is incorrect.** “In addition,” would add another general point of similar scope, but the coin-flip sentence is a concrete example, not a separate general claim, so this transition is too broad.
**Choice B is incorrect.** “However,” signals contrast, but the coin-flip statistic supports rather than contradicts the preceding statement, so contrast is inappropriate.
**Choice D is incorrect.** “In comparison,” sets up a comparison between two things; the coin-flip sentence is an example of the same phenomenon, not a comparative statement, so this transition misrepresents the relationship.
**Faster approach:** Look at the role of the next sentence (example vs. contrast vs. addition); choose the transition that matches that role—here, pick an example introducer like “For instance.”'),
  ('2e170a64-bcaf-5cd7-9d5b-e88cb502d258', 3, null, '**Choice D is correct.** The sentence sets up a clear contrast between elevation above sea level (topographic maps) and elevation below the sea; the phrase "by contrast" signals that the second clause will present a contrasting idea, which fits the parallel structure and clarifies the difference between the two types of maps.
**Choice A is incorrect.** "Afterward" indicates sequence in time, which does not match the intended comparison between maps showing above- versus below-sea-level elevations.
**Choice B is incorrect.** "In conclusion" signals a summary or ending, which is inappropriate because the sentence is defining and contrasting two related terms, not concluding an argument.
**Choice C is incorrect.** "For example" would introduce an instance, but the second clause is not an example of topographic maps; it is a contrasting definition of a different kind of map.
**Faster approach:** Look for signal words in the surrounding text (here "above" vs "below") and choose the transition that indicates contrast.'),
  ('219cc443-68e1-50d3-8798-1188ea9d704a', 1, null, '**Choice B is correct.** "Specifically," signals that the second sentence is giving a more detailed example of how digital artists use edge modeling, which directly follows the general statement in the first sentence. The second sentence narrows the topic by explaining a particular use: piecing together separate shapes to create 3D objects for games.
**Choice A is incorrect.** "In addition," would indicate an extra, separate point about edge modeling, but the second sentence is not adding a separate point—it is elaborating on the first sentence.
**Choice C is incorrect.** "Thus," indicates a result or conclusion; the second sentence does not present a consequence but a specific explanation of use, so "thus" is inappropriate.
**Choice D is incorrect.** "By comparison," would set up a contrast with something else, but no contrasting element is presented; the second sentence instead gives a specific example related to the first.
**Faster approach:** Look for whether the second sentence narrows or expands the first. If it provides a specific example or elaboration, choose "Specifically."'),
  ('92d449c0-68c7-5fbb-ad5d-a5517873737d', 3, null, '**Choice D is correct.** The sentence compares Indonesia’s change in urban population with the regional change over the same five-year period, so a transition that signals simultaneity is required. "Concurrently," accurately indicates that the increase in Indonesia occurred at the same time that urbanization rates rose across Southeast Asia by 2.8 percentage points.
**Choice A is incorrect.** "For example," would introduce an instance after a general statement; here the passage moves from a specific country to the regional trend, so "for example" is inappropriate.
**Choice B is incorrect.** "Eventually," implies an outcome occurring later in time, which does not fit the immediate, simultaneous comparison between the two five-year changes.
**Choice C is incorrect.** "In other words" would signal a restatement or clarification of the same idea, but the sentence presents a related regional statistic, not a rephrasing of the Indonesia figure.
**Faster approach:** Identify whether the second clause shows simultaneity, example, restatement, or sequence; choose the transition that matches—here only "concurrently" signals simultaneous regional change.'),
  ('e8fd0452-e0a8-518f-9bff-88f387a576df', 3, null, '**Choice D is correct.** The notes state that 87% of the world’s languages are either SVO or SOV. In both SVO (subject–verb–object) and SOV (subject–object–verb) orders the subject appears before the verb (and before the object in SOV), so the statistic supports the generalization that a majority of languages place the subject before the verb and object.
**Choice A is incorrect.** This restates that word order is a structural feature used by typologists but does not make the desired generalization about how word order is employed across languages.
**Choice B is incorrect.** That choice describes what typologists do (analyzing and comparing) rather than making a general claim about how word order is distributed across languages.
**Choice C is incorrect.** Choice C simply defines the term “word order” (a restatement of a note) rather than drawing a generalization about patterns across languages.
**Faster approach:** Find the statistic (87%) and connect it to the definitions of SVO and SOV to translate the percentage into a general statement about subject placement; this quickly yields the correct generalization every time.'),
  ('02ed2ec2-c743-58e7-9162-3826b0f0df91', 1, null, '**Choice B is correct.** The notes state that Pbact was judged "the most effective" because it alone combined a sufficiently high yield with a purity near the ideal 1.8; that implies the other kits (CTAB 2X and Pplant) were judged less effective because each lacked either an acceptable yield or acceptable purity. The sentence in choice B accurately synthesizes those details and emphasizes the shared status (being less effective) of CTAB 2X and Pplant while also attributing the correct reasons (insufficient yield or purity).
**Choice A is incorrect.** Although the numeric claim is true (CTAB 2X: 2134 ng/μL and purity 2.2; Pplant: 12.5 ng/μL and purity 1.5), the choice merely lists two facts rather than emphasizing the significance of a shared evaluative conclusion from the researchers.
**Choice C is incorrect.** CTAB 2X has purity 2.2 and Pbact has purity 1.6, so they are not equal in purity.
**Choice D is incorrect.** The claim that both Pbact and Pplant had insufficient yields contradicts the researchers'' evaluation that Pbact had a sufficiently high yield; Pbact’s yield (157 ng/μL) was considered adequate.
**Faster approach:** Identify the researchers'' overall judgment (which kit they called "most effective") and then choose the answer that best states why the others were grouped together (insufficient yield or purity) rather than focusing on isolated numbers.')
on conflict (question_id) do update set correct_index = excluded.correct_index, correct_answers = excluded.correct_answers,
  explanation = excluded.explanation;

